; ======================================================================
; math.pico2 - the desktop BASIC maths functions, in 32-bit binary float,
;              ON THE RP2350
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;     EnableFloatingPoint                       ; REQUIRED - see below
;     XIncludeFile "RP2350/Lib/math.pico2"
;
;     Procedure Main()
;       Protected h.f
;       h = Sqr(3.0 * 3.0 + 4.0 * 4.0)          ; 5.0
;       h = Sin(Radian(30.0))                   ; 0.5
;       h = ATan2(1.0, 1.0)                     ; 0.7853982  (45 degrees)
;       h = Pow(2.0, 10.0)                      ; 1024.0
;     EndProcedure
;
; Sqr Pow Sin Cos Tan ATan2 Log Exp Int Degree Radian Sign, plus the four
; special-value helpers NaN Infinity IsNaN IsInfinity that the domain
; answers below are stated in terms of.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PORT OF RP2040/Lib/math.pico, AND THE ONE THING THAT WAS CHECKED
; ======================================================================
; Every executable line below is character-for-character the RP2040
; file's. That is a RESULT, not a starting assumption: the vault ruled on
; 2026-08-02 that the RP2350 never shares RP2040/Lib, because a file that
; pokes RP2040 addresses and a file that is genuinely portable read the
; same from the include line. So this one was read whole and searched for
; the specific chip fact it was most likely to be smuggling.
;
; THE SIO HARDWARE DIVIDER, AND WHY IT TURNED OUT NOT TO MATTER HERE.
; The RP2040 has a hardware divider in SIO at $D0000060..$D0000078. The
; RP2350 HAS NONE - that address range is reserved on this chip
; (Help/RP2350.txt, corrected 2026-08-06; the M33 divides with its own
; udiv/sdiv instructions, which this backend does not emit yet either).
; So a ported file that reached the divider would compile or refuse
; depending on how it reached it, and the frightening case would be a
; silent wrong answer.
;
; It cannot happen, for two independent reasons, and BOTH were checked
; rather than reasoned about:
;
;   1. NOTHING IN THIS FILE NAMES THE DIVIDER. sio_divide() and
;      sio_modulo() appear nowhere in it, and nowhere in any of the nine
;      libraries ported alongside it. The RP2040 callers of those
;      intrinsics are elsewhere - MathLib/q16.pico's QDiv,
;      q16_random.pico, and the Pico_HardwareDivide example - and none of
;      them is this file.
;
;   2. '/' AND '%' NEVER LOWERED ONTO THE DIVIDER ANYWAY, on either chip.
;      RP2040/the compiler's RP2040IntMath module says so in its own banner: the hardware
;      divide block "is still dormant", and "'/' and '%' lower to
;      __pm_divmod, the software restoring loop in the compiler's ARMFloatHelpers module, on
;      every RP2040 program." The RP2350 reuses that same emitter. So the
;      operators in this file mean the same instructions on both parts.
;
;   And the failure mode if either had been wrong is the good one:
;   sio_divide and sio_modulo are declared in rp2040_hardware.def and are
;   ABSENT from RP2350/Intrinsics/rp2350_hardware.def, so naming one here
;   stops the build by name. There is no way to reach a divider that is
;   not there and get a plausible number back.
;
; NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE TOUCHES HARDWARE. It is .f arithmetic, bit
; patterns read through PeekL/PokeL of a local, and polynomials.
;
; ======================================================================
; REQUIRES
; ======================================================================
;   EnableFloatingPoint, in YOUR file, ABOVE the XIncludeFile line.
;
;   Includes no other file (house rule: a library never includes a
;   library - the MAIN file lists them). It needs none: .f arithmetic is
;   built into the compiler on this target, so everything here is
;   ordinary code. It compiles standalone for rp2350 when the pragma is
;   supplied.
;
;   The pragma is not optional and the error you get without it does not
;   say so. This file is .f arithmetic throughout and contains about
;   ninety fractional literals. Without the pragma each one is typed as
;   Q32.32 FIXED-POINT - that is the default - and cannot be combined
;   with the .f variables here, so the build stops with a message about
;   mixing .fx and .f that names a line INSIDE THIS LIBRARY and mentions
;   nothing you wrote. serial_print_f32 on the AVR line has the identical
;   requirement for the identical reason.
;
;   .fx IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT ON THIS TARGET - Help/RP2350.txt lists
;   ".fx / .q / .dec / .d (8-byte types are not lowered on ARM)" among
;   the refusals - so on the Pico 2 a missing pragma is even more likely
;   to produce a message about a type you never asked for.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT PART OF A WIRING LIBRARY
; ======================================================================
; wiring.pico2 carries min/max/abs/constrain/map: the Arduino surface,
; whole numbers, no pragma, cheap enough that a blink sketch can carry
; them. Everything here is binary32 and needs EnableFloatingPoint. Fold
; the two together and every program that only wanted constrain() starts
; paying the float pragma and the float helpers.
;
; It is also a different NAMESPACE with different rules. These are the
; desktop BASIC's names and the desktop BASIC's exact semantics, argument
; order included - ATan2 takes x FIRST, which is the reverse of the C
; function of the same name and is the single most likely thing to be
; got wrong here. Mixing that contract into a file documented as "the
; Arduino API" would leave a reader with no way to know which set of
; rules a given name follows.
;
; ======================================================================
; Abs IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
; ======================================================================
; The RP2040's wiring.pico defines abs(v.i) and identifiers fold case, so
; an Abs(x.f) here would make the two files refuse to be included
; together - for one function that is two lines to write inline:
;
;     If v < 0.0 : v = 0.0 - v : EndIf
;
; Same reasoning wiring_math.avrb gives for leaving bitSet() to bits.avrb,
; and the same reason bits.pico2 next door does not define one either.
;
; ======================================================================
; SINGLE PRECISION ONLY, AND WHAT THAT COSTS YOU
; ======================================================================
; The desktop BASIC computes these in DOUBLE and offers a .d overload.
; This language has .f and no .d on this target - and .d is refused
; outright here, see REQUIRES - so every function is binary32 in and
; binary32 out. binary32 carries about 7 significant decimal digits. A
; result that agrees with the desktop to 7 digits is agreeing as closely
; as the type can.
;
; THE RP2350 HAS AN FPU AND THIS FILE DOES NOT USE IT. The Cortex-M33 on
; this part has single-precision floating point in hardware; Help/
; RP2350.txt lists "the FPU" among the things this target does NOT yet
; use, because the backend reuses the RP2040's Thumb-1 encoder and
; Thumb-1 has no VFP encodings. So every .f operation below goes through
; the same software helpers in the compiler's ARMFloatHelpers module that serve the RP2040.
; That is slower than the silicon can go, and it is the reason the
; accuracy table below transfers - see ACCURACY.
;
; Where a function CANNOT match the desktop it is written down here and
; in that function's own comment, never left to be discovered:
;
;   Int()    returns a 32-bit value; the desktop returns the machine's
;            native integer, which is 64-bit on a 64-bit build. Magnitudes
;            above 2147483647 saturate here. (32 bits is the pointer and
;            integer width on this chip too - 520 KB of SRAM instead of
;            264 KB moves the address RANGE, not the WIDTH.)
;   Sin Cos  refuse an argument beyond 12867 radians instead of returning
;   Tan      a plausible number. The desktop keeps answering; its answers
;            out there are wrong too, just quietly. See TRIG below.
;   Pow      for a non-integer exponent is exp(y*log(x)) and carries that
;            composition's error, about 7e-6 relative rather than the
;            type's own 6e-8 - see the 113 ULP figure under ACCURACY.
;            An INTEGER exponent up to +/-64 takes a repeated-squaring
;            path instead. A NEGATIVE base is refused past that bound -
;            see Pow(), where the measurement is.
;
; ======================================================================
; DOMAIN ERRORS - EVERY ONE HAS A DEFINED, DOCUMENTED ANSWER
; ======================================================================
; These match the desktop BASIC, which was RUN to establish them rather
; than read out of a manual:
;
;     Sqr(-1)          NaN            Log(0)        -Infinity
;     Sqr(-0.0)        -0.0           Log(-1)       NaN
;     Sqr(+Inf)        +Infinity      Log(+Inf)     +Infinity
;     Exp(1000)        +Infinity      Exp(-1000)    0.0
;     Pow(0, 0)        1.0            Pow(0, -1)    +Infinity
;     Pow(-2, 3)       -8.0           Pow(-2, 0.5)  NaN
;     Pow(x, 0)        1.0 always     ATan2(0, 0)   0.0
;     Sign(NaN)        0              anything(NaN) NaN
;
; Every one of those rows is an ASSERTION in
; RP2350/Examples/Verification/rp2350LibSelfTest.pico2, checked on this
; target rather than inherited on trust.
;
; The two places this file DIFFERS from the desktop, both deliberate:
;
;   Int(NaN) is 0 here. The desktop returns the CPU's "integer
;   indefinite" (-9223372036854775808), which is a plausible-looking
;   number that is not an answer. 0 is not an answer either, but it is
;   not a number anyone will mistake for one - and Sign()/IsNaN() are
;   right there if you need to ask.
;
;   Sin/Cos/Tan return NaN beyond 12867 radians. See TRIG.
;
; ======================================================================
; TRIG: THE RANGE LIMIT IS A REFUSAL, AND IT IS THE POINT
; ======================================================================
; Reducing an angle modulo pi/2 is where a float trig function actually
; goes wrong. pi/2 is carried here in THREE pieces so that each product
; n*piece is exact:
;
;     A = 1.5703125                    = 201 * 2^-7,  8 significant bits
;     B = 0.0004837512969970703125     11 significant bits
;     C = 0.0000000754979012640433     the remainder
;
; n*A stays exact while 201*|n| < 2^24 and n*B while 2029*|n| < 2^24. The
; second is the binding one: |n| < 8267, i.e. |x| < about 12986 radians.
; Past that the reduction quietly loses bits and the error grows with no
; outward sign at all, so beyond 12867 radians (2048 whole turns) these
; return NaN. A NaN propagates and can be tested for; a sine that is
; wrong in the fourth digit cannot.
;
; If you are accumulating an angle in a loop, wrap it as you go rather
; than letting it grow - Radian(deg % 360) costs nothing.
;
; ======================================================================
; NO GLOBALS, SO THESE ARE REENTRANT
; ======================================================================
; Every value lives in a Protected local, including the ones only there
; to be looked at bit by bit. That is not free - it costs a frame slot
; and a couple of instructions per call - and it is bought deliberately:
; a maths function that keeps its scratch in a file-level global cannot
; be called from an interrupt while the main program is inside it, and
; the failure when you do is a wrong number rather than a crash.
;
; ON THIS PART IT BUYS A SECOND THING, and it is worth naming because the
; RP2350 file is where it first matters: interrupts WORK on this target
; (Help/RP2350.txt - first interrupt on silicon 2026-08-06,
; rp2350SysTickTest.pico2), with 52 IRQ lines and the M33's own system
; exception slots. A handler that wants a square root can have one.
; string_f32.pico2's StrF is the counter-example next door: it keeps its
; scratch at file level and says so, and must not be called from a
; handler while the foreground is inside it.
;
; serial_print_f32.avrb keeps its scratch in globals for a reason that no
; longer applies: taking '@' of a frame-relative local was not known to
; work on that backend. It was measured on all three backends on
; 2026-08-03 and works everywhere, which is what made this file possible.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IT COSTS
; ======================================================================
; The RP2040 measurement was:
;
;     a program with none of this               1900 bytes
;     the same program including this file     10704 bytes   (+8804)
;     ... and calling Sqr as well              10724 bytes   (+20)
;
; READ THAT SECOND NUMBER CAREFULLY: on this backend the include costs
; the WHOLE FILE whether you call anything or not, and the call itself is
; then nearly free. Dead-code stripping exists in this compiler but only
; the AVR backends have opted into it, so what you include here is what
; you get. Roughly nine kilobytes is 0.2% of the 4 MB flash the RP2350
; images are linked against and does not matter; the same file is refused
; on an ATmega328P for exactly the reason this number would matter there.
;
; The figures above are the RP2040's and have NOT been re-run on this
; part. The exact byte count on the Pico 2 is whatever the build says;
; the shape of the statement - all of it, always - is what carries over,
; and that is a property of the compiler, not of the chip.
;
; ======================================================================
; ACCURACY, MEASURED - ON THE RP2040, AND WHY THAT TRANSFERS
; ======================================================================
; READ THE PROVENANCE BEFORE THE NUMBERS. Every figure below came from
; RUNNING the compiled image ON THE RP2040 and scoring the printed bit
; patterns against the same expression in double precision - 1300
; pseudo-random arguments per line, not a handful of hand-picked ones.
; NONE OF IT HAS BEEN RE-RUN ON A PICO 2, and this file does not claim
; otherwise.
;
; What makes them transferable is not optimism, it is that the ARITHMETIC
; IS THE SAME OBJECT CODE: this target does not use the M33's FPU (see
; SINGLE PRECISION above), so every add, multiply, divide and compare
; below goes through the identical software routines in
; the compiler's ARMFloatHelpers module that produced these numbers on the RP2040. Same
; helpers, same polynomial, same binary32 grid - the results are bit
; patterns, and bit patterns do not depend on how fast the core is.
;
; That is an argument, and the project's own rule is that an argument is
; not a measurement. So the self-test checks the ANSWERS this file
; promises - the whole DOMAIN ERRORS table, Sqr(2), Sin/Cos/Tan at known
; angles, Pow's two paths, the trig refusal past 12867 - on this part.
; What it does NOT re-establish is the ULP tables; the day somebody wants
; those on a Pico 2, the 1300-argument harness has to be run here.
;
;     Sqr        1 ULP     x spread over the whole finite positive range
;     Exp        1 ULP     -87 <= x <= 88
;     Log        1 ULP     x spread over the whole finite positive range
;     Sin Cos    1 ULP     |x| <= 2*pi, and also |x| <= 12867
;     Tan        3 ULP     |x| <= 1.4
;     ATan2      1 ULP     both arguments spread over 1e-9 .. 1e+9,
;                          all four quadrants
;     Pow        9 ULP     whole-number exponent, |y| <= 20
;                113 ULP   fractional exponent - 7.2e-06 RELATIVE, which
;                          is the honest way to read it. See Pow().
;
; TRIG NEAR A ZERO IS THE ONE PLACE ULPs MISLEAD, so it is measured
; separately: with arguments deliberately placed within 0.001 radians of
; a multiple of pi, out to 4000*pi, Sin is 52 ULP and Cos 974 ULP - and
; the ABSOLUTE error at those points is 1.4e-10 and 2.1e-10. Both
; statements are true and they are about the same numbers: a result of
; 3e-7 has almost no significant bits left, so a fixed absolute error
; counts as an enormous number of steps. If you are testing sin(x) for a
; zero crossing, compare against a small absolute tolerance and not
; against 0.0 - which is true of every implementation of these functions
; and not a property of this one.
;
; NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS RUN ON SILICON. It compiles for rp2350 and its
; answers are checked by rp2350LibSelfTest.pico2 under tools/arm_run.exe.
; ======================================================================
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FloatBits(x) - the raw IEEE-754 binary32 bit pattern of x, as a signed
; 32-bit value. Nothing is converted: FloatBits(1.5) is 1069547520, not 1.
; This is how every classification in this file is done - a NaN or an
; infinity has to be recognised from its bits, because arithmetic on one
; produces another one and tells you nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l FloatBits(x.f)
  Protected t.f
  t = x
  ProcedureReturn PeekL(@t)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FloatFromBits(b) - the binary32 value whose bit pattern is b. The exact
; inverse of FloatBits, and again nothing is converted: FloatFromBits(1)
; is the smallest subnormal, not 1.0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f FloatFromBits(b.l)
  Protected t.f
  PokeL(@t, b)
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Infinity() - positive infinity, 0x7F800000. Negate it for the other one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Infinity()
  ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2139095040)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NaN() - a quiet Not-a-Number. Every domain error in this file returns
; this one bit pattern, 0x7FC00000.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f NaN()
  ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2143289344)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IsNaN(x) - 1 if x is Not-a-Number, else 0.
; Do NOT test for a NaN with '=': a NaN compares unequal to everything
; including itself, so "If x = NaN()" is false even when x IS a NaN. That
; property is in fact what this uses - x <> x is true for a NaN and for
; nothing else - but relying on it in your own code reads as a typo, so
; call this instead.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l IsNaN(x.f)
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IsInfinity(x) - 1 for +Infinity, -1 for -Infinity, 0 for anything else
; including NaN. The desktop BASIC only promises "nonzero", so a program
; that just tests truth is portable; the sign is extra.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l IsInfinity(x.f)
  Protected b.l
  b = FloatBits(x)
  If b = 2139095040
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If b = -8388608                   ; 0xFF800000 read as a signed .l
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sign(x) - -1 if x is negative, +1 if positive, 0 if zero.
; Returns an INTEGER, as the desktop's does. Sign(-0.0) is 0 and
; Sign(NaN) is 0; both were checked against the desktop rather than
; assumed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l Sign(x.f)
  If x <> x                         ; NaN - not positive, not negative
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If x > 0.0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If x < 0.0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Int(x) - the integer part of x, TRUNCATED toward zero. Int(10.565) is
; 10 and Int(-10.565) is -10, which is the desktop's rule and NOT the
; older BASIC floor() rule that would give -11. There is no rounding.
;
; TWO DIFFERENCES FROM THE DESKTOP, both forced:
;   - the result is 32-bit. A magnitude beyond 2147483647 saturates at
;     +/-2147483647 rather than wrapping, because a wrapped answer looks
;     like a real one. The desktop returns a 64-bit value and its IntQ()
;     has no counterpart here.
;   - Int(NaN) is 0. The desktop hands back the CPU's integer-indefinite
;     value, -9223372036854775808, which is not an answer either but is
;     far more likely to be mistaken for one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l Int(x.f)
  Protected n.l
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If x >= 2147483648.0
    ProcedureReturn 2147483647
  EndIf
  If x <= -2147483648.0
    ProcedureReturn -2147483647
  EndIf
  n = x                             ; the backend's f32->s32 truncates toward zero
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Degree(x) - an angle in radians converted to degrees. Degree(#PI/4) is
; 45.0. There is NO normalisation: an input above 2*pi gives a result
; above 360, and a negative input gives a negative result, exactly as the
; desktop does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Degree(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn x * 57.29577951308232
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Radian(x) - an angle in degrees converted to radians. Radian(90) is
; 1.5707964. No normalisation, same as Degree().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Radian(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn x * 0.017453292519943295
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathNearestInt(t) - t rounded to the NEAREST integer, halves away from
; zero. Used by the range reductions below, where an off-by-one quadrant
; is a sign error rather than a rounding error.
;
; It is spelled out through a local rather than written as
; "ProcedureReturn t + 0.5" because the conversion then happens at the
; return, and a float-to-int conversion buried in a return statement is
; the shape that used to be silently skipped on the ARM backends. Being
; explicit costs nothing and cannot be misread.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l MathNearestInt(t.f)
  Protected u.f
  Protected n.l
  If t >= 0.0
    u = t + 0.5
  Else
    u = t - 0.5
  EndIf
  n = u
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathPow2i(n) - 2 raised to an INTEGER power, built directly from the
; exponent field rather than by multiplying. Exact for -126 <= n <= 127;
; 0.0 below that and +Infinity above. Exp() and Sqr() use it to apply a
; power-of-two scaling, which is the one scaling that costs no accuracy
; at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathPow2i(n.l)
  If n > 127
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2139095040)
  EndIf
  If n < -126
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits((n + 127) << 23)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sqr(x) - the square root of x.
;
;     Sqr(-1)     NaN          Sqr(0.0)     0.0
;     Sqr(-0.0)   -0.0         Sqr(+Inf)    +Infinity
;
; MEASURED 1 ULP worst case over 1300 arguments spread across the whole
; finite positive range.
;
; METHOD. A first estimate is read straight out of the exponent field:
; halving a float's bit pattern halves its exponent, which is most of a
; square root already, and the additive constant 532487669 is the offset
; that minimises the leading error of what is left (about 3.5%). Three
; Newton-Raphson steps y = (y + x/y)/2 follow, each roughly doubling the
; correct digits: 3.5% -> 6e-4 -> 2e-7 -> below one ULP. A fourth step
; would change nothing a binary32 can hold.
;
; A SUBNORMAL INPUT IS SCALED FIRST. Below 1.18e-38 the exponent field is
; 0 and the bit trick has nothing to halve, so the estimate is garbage
; and Newton converges to garbage - silently, because every step still
; looks like it is converging. Multiplying by 2^40 first and the result
; by 2^-20 is exact both ways (powers of two) and puts the value back in
; the range the estimate was fitted for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Sqr(x.f)
  Protected b.l
  Protected v.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected q.f
  Protected scale.f
  If x <> x                         ; NaN in, NaN out
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If x < 0.0
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  If x = 0.0                        ; returns x, so -0.0 stays -0.0
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  b = FloatBits(x)
  If b = 2139095040                 ; +Infinity
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  v = x
  scale = 1.0
  If b < 8388608                    ; subnormal - see the note above
    v = v * 1099511627776.0         ; 2^40
    scale = 0.00000095367431640625  ; 2^-20
    b = FloatBits(v)
  EndIf
  y = FloatFromBits((b >> 1) + 532487669)
  q = v / y
  y = (y + q) * 0.5
  q = v / y
  y = (y + q) * 0.5
  q = v / y
  y = (y + q) * 0.5
  ProcedureReturn y * scale
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Exp(x) - e raised to the power x. The inverse of Log().
;
;     Exp(0)      1.0          Exp(1000)    +Infinity
;     Exp(-1000)  0.0          Exp(NaN)     NaN
;
; MEASURED 1 ULP worst case over 1300 arguments in -87 <= x <= 88.
;
; METHOD. x is split as x = n*ln2 + r with n whole and |r| <= ln2/2, so
; e^x = 2^n * e^r and only the small factor needs a polynomial. ln2 is
; carried in two pieces - 0.693359375 is 355/512, nine significant bits,
; so n*it is EXACT for every n that can occur here, and the tiny
; correction 0.00021219444005469057 restores the rest. Subtracting one
; exact product loses nothing; subtracting a single rounded ln2 would
; lose about a bit per unit of |x|, which is the usual way this function
; is got wrong.
;
; e^r on |r| <= 0.3466 is a degree-7 minimax polynomial evaluated as
; 1 + r + p(r)*r^2, where p is the degree-5 Horner chain below - which
; keeps the two dominant terms exact and lets the polynomial carry only
; the small remainder.
;
; The 2^n factor is applied last with MathPow2i, one exact multiply. When
; n is below -126 the answer is subnormal and 2^n is not representable,
; so the scaling is done in two steps rather than flushed to zero -
; without that, exp(-90) would come out as 0 instead of 8.2e-40.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Exp(x.f)
  Protected n.l
  Protected t.f
  Protected nf.f
  Protected r.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected v.f
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If x > 88.72283                   ; the largest x with a finite e^x
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2139095040)
  EndIf
  If x < -103.9721                  ; below the smallest subnormal
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  t = x * 1.4426950408889634        ; x / ln2
  n = MathNearestInt(t)
  nf = n
  r = x - nf * 0.693359375          ; exact: 355/512
  r = r + nf * 0.00021219444005469057
  p = 0.000198756915
  p = p * r + 0.0013981999507
  p = p * r + 0.0083334519073
  p = p * r + 0.041665795894
  p = p * r + 0.16666665459
  p = p * r + 0.50000001201
  z = r * r
  v = 1.0 + r
  v = v + p * z
  If n < -126
    v = v * MathPow2i(n + 60)
    ProcedureReturn v * MathPow2i(-60)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v * MathPow2i(n)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Log(x) - the natural logarithm (base e) of x. The inverse of Exp().
;
;     Log(1)      0.0          Log(0)       -Infinity
;     Log(-1)     NaN          Log(+Inf)    +Infinity
;
; MEASURED 1 ULP worst case over 1300 arguments spread across the whole
; finite positive range.
;
; METHOD. x is split into m * 2^e with m in [sqrt(1/2), sqrt(2)) - the
; exponent comes straight out of the bit pattern, so that half is exact -
; and log(x) = e*ln2 + log(m). log(m) is then evaluated as the atanh
; series in s = (m-1)/(m+1):
;
;     log(m) = 2s * (1 + s^2/3 + s^4/5 + s^6/7 + s^8/9)
;
; which is used instead of a series in (m-1) because |s| <= 0.1716 while
; |m-1| can reach 0.414 - the terms fall away about six times faster, so
; five of them reach 7e-10 where a direct series would need fifteen.
; Centring m on 1 rather than leaving it in [1,2) is what buys that; it
; costs one comparison and one exact halving.
;
; A SUBNORMAL INPUT is scaled up by 2^40 first and 40*ln2 subtracted from
; the exponent term afterwards, for the same reason as in Sqr(): a
; subnormal has no exponent field to read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Log(x.f)
  Protected b.l
  Protected e.l
  Protected v.f
  Protected m.f
  Protected s.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected ef.f
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If x < 0.0
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  If x = 0.0
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(-8388608)     ; -Infinity
  EndIf
  b = FloatBits(x)
  If b = 2139095040
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  v = x
  e = 0
  If b < 8388608                    ; subnormal - see the note above
    v = v * 1099511627776.0         ; 2^40
    e = -40
    b = FloatBits(v)
  EndIf
  e = e + ((b >> 23) & 255) - 127
  m = FloatFromBits((b & 8388607) | 1065353216)   ; mantissa, exponent forced to 0
  If m > 1.4142135623730951
    m = m * 0.5
    e = e + 1
  EndIf
  s = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0)
  z = s * s
  p = 0.1111111111111111
  p = p * z + 0.14285714285714285
  p = p * z + 0.2
  p = p * z + 0.3333333333333333
  p = p * z + 1.0
  ef = e
  ProcedureReturn ef * 0.6931471805599453 + 2.0 * s * p
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathSinCos(x, wantCos) - the shared core of Sin() and Cos().
; wantCos of 0 gives sin(x), anything else gives cos(x).
;
; Both are one function because the argument reduction IS the function -
; the polynomial afterwards is a few instructions and the reduction is
; everything that decides whether the answer is right. Two copies of it
; would be two things to keep in step, and the one that drifts would go
; wrong only at large arguments, which is exactly where nobody looks.
;
; A cosine is a sine one quadrant along, so wantCos simply adds 1 to the
; quadrant count. The quadrant then selects sin(r) or cos(r) of the
; reduced argument and whether to negate it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathSinCos(x.f, wantCos.l)
  Protected a.f
  Protected t.f
  Protected nf.f
  Protected r.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected v.f
  Protected n.l
  Protected q.l
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  a = x
  If a < 0.0
    a = 0.0 - a
  EndIf
  If a > 12867.0                    ; the reduction stops being exact - refuse
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  t = x * 0.6366197723675814        ; x * 2/pi - which quadrant
  n = MathNearestInt(t)
  nf = n
  r = x - nf * 1.5703125            ; pi/2 in three exact pieces
  r = r - nf * 0.0004837512969970703125
  r = r - nf * 0.0000000754979012640433
  q = n & 3
  If wantCos <> 0
    q = (q + 1) & 3
  EndIf
  z = r * r
  If q = 1 Or q = 3
    ; cos(r) = 1 - r^2/2 + r^4/24 - ... , Taylor to r^10; the truncated
    ; term is (pi/4)^12/479001600, about 1.2e-10, well under one ULP.
    p = -0.0000002755731922398589
    p = p * z + 0.0000248015873015873
    p = p * z + -0.001388888888888889
    p = p * z + 0.041666666666666664
    p = p * z + -0.5
    v = 1.0 + p * z
  Else
    ; sin(r) = r - r^3/6 + r^5/120 - ... , Taylor to r^9; the truncated
    ; term is (pi/4)^11/39916800, about 1.9e-09.
    p = 0.0000027557319223985893
    p = p * z + -0.0001984126984126984
    p = p * z + 0.008333333333333333
    p = p * z + -0.16666666666666666
    v = r + r * (p * z)
  EndIf
  If q >= 2
    ProcedureReturn 0.0 - v
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sin(x) - the sine of an angle given in RADIANS. Use Radian() to convert
; from degrees. The result is between -1.0 and 1.0.
;
;     Sin(0)          0.0
;     Sin(Radian(30)) 0.5
;     Sin(NaN)        NaN
;     |x| > 12867     NaN     - a refusal, not a failure. See TRIG above.
;
; MEASURED 1 ULP worst case over 1300 arguments in |x| <= 2*pi and 1300
; more in |x| <= 12867. Within 0.001 radians of a multiple of pi the
; ABSOLUTE error is 1.4e-10 and the ULP count is meaningless - see the
; note at the top of the file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Sin(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathSinCos(x, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Cos(x) - the cosine of an angle given in RADIANS. Result -1.0 to 1.0.
; Same accuracy, same 12867-radian refusal and same reasoning as Sin().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Cos(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathSinCos(x, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Tan(x) - the tangent of an angle given in RADIANS.
;
; Computed as Sin(x)/Cos(x), and the consequences of that are worth
; stating rather than hiding. Near a pole (an odd multiple of pi/2) the
; cosine is tiny and its few remaining significant bits decide the whole
; answer, so accuracy degrades steadily as you approach one - away from a
; pole this is about 3 ULP, at 1e-4 radians from a pole it is nearer 1e-3
; relative. That is the identity's arithmetic, not a defect, and the
; desktop BASIC behaves the same way: it returns 37320540 for
; Tan(1.5707963) because that argument is not exactly pi/2 either.
;
; Cos(x) can never be exactly 0 for a representable x, since pi/2 is not
; representable - but if it ever were, the answer would be the correctly
; signed Infinity, which is also right.
;
;     Tan(0)       0.0        |x| > 12867   NaN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Tan(x.f)
  Protected s.f
  Protected c.f
  s = MathSinCos(x, 0)
  c = MathSinCos(x, 1)
  ProcedureReturn s / c
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathATan(t) - arctangent of t, in radians, over the whole real line.
; Result between -pi/2 and +pi/2. Internal; ATan2() is the public one.
;
; The argument is folded twice before the polynomial so that it only ever
; has to cover |z| <= tan(pi/8) = 0.4142:
;
;     |t| > 2.4142   z = -1/t      and add pi/2   (atan t = pi/2 - atan 1/t)
;     |t| > 0.4142   z = (t-1)/(t+1) and add pi/4
;
; Both identities are exact rearrangements, and folding twice is what
; makes a four-coefficient minimax fit enough. Without it the series near
; |t| = 1 converges so slowly that fifteen terms would not do.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathATan(t.f)
  Protected a.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected w.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected y0.f
  Protected v.f
  Protected neg.l
  neg = 0
  a = t
  If a < 0.0
    a = 0.0 - a
    neg = 1
  EndIf
  If a > 2.414213562373095
    z = -1.0 / a
    y0 = 1.5707963267948966
  ElseIf a > 0.4142135623730950
    z = (a - 1.0) / (a + 1.0)
    y0 = 0.7853981633974483
  Else
    z = a
    y0 = 0.0
  EndIf
  w = z * z
  p = 0.0805374449538
  p = p * w + -0.138776856032
  p = p * w + 0.199777106478
  p = p * w + -0.333329491539
  v = y0 + (z + z * (p * w))
  If neg <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0.0 - v
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ATan2(x, y) - the angle in radians between the x axis and the direction
; (x, y). Result between -pi and +pi.
;
; ================ THE X COMES FIRST. READ THIS TWICE. =================
; The desktop BASIC spells this ATan2(x, y) and computes the arctangent
; of y/x. Every C library, and everything that copies C, spells the same
; function atan2(y, x) - the arguments the other way round. This file
; follows the desktop, because that is the language these names come
; from. Swapping them does not fail; it silently reflects every angle
; about the 45-degree line, which is a bug people find on a robot rather
; than on a bench.
;
;     ATan2(1, 0)   0.0            ATan2(0, 1)    +pi/2
;     ATan2(-1, 0)  +pi            ATan2(0, -1)   -pi/2
;     ATan2(10, 10) +pi/4          ATan2(0, 0)    0.0
;
; ATan2(0, 0) is 0.0, which is a DEFINITION, not a computation - the
; direction of a zero-length vector is not a question with an answer, and
; 0.0 is the answer the desktop gives, so it is the answer given here.
;
; Infinite arguments are handled explicitly rather than left to fall out
; of the division: y/x with both infinite is a NaN, and a NaN there would
; be a wrong answer for a direction that is perfectly well defined.
;
; MEASURED 1 ULP worst case over 1300 argument pairs, all four quadrants,
; magnitudes from 1e-9 to 1e+9.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f ATan2(x.f, y.f)
  Protected ix.l
  Protected iy.l
  Protected r.f
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If y <> y
    ProcedureReturn y
  EndIf
  ix = IsInfinity(x)
  iy = IsInfinity(y)
  If ix <> 0 Or iy <> 0
    If ix > 0 And iy = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0.0
    EndIf
    If ix < 0 And iy = 0
      If y < 0.0
        ProcedureReturn -3.141592653589793
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn 3.141592653589793
    EndIf
    If ix = 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    If ix = 0 And iy < 0
      ProcedureReturn -1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    ; both infinite: the direction is one of the four diagonals
    If ix > 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 0.7853981633974483
    EndIf
    If ix > 0 And iy < 0
      ProcedureReturn -0.7853981633974483
    EndIf
    If ix < 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 2.356194490192345
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn -2.356194490192345
  EndIf
  If x = 0.0
    If y > 0.0
      ProcedureReturn 1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    If y < 0.0
      ProcedureReturn -1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0.0             ; ATan2(0, 0) - defined as 0.0
  EndIf
  r = MathATan(y / x)
  If x > 0.0
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If y < 0.0
    ProcedureReturn r - 3.141592653589793
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r + 3.141592653589793
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathPowInt(x, k) - x raised to a whole number power, by repeated
; squaring. Internal; Pow() routes to it whenever it can.
; k may be negative; k of 0 gives 1.0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathPowInt(x.f, k.l)
  Protected base.f
  Protected acc.f
  Protected e.l
  acc = 1.0
  base = x
  e = k
  If e < 0
    e = 0 - e
  EndIf
  While e > 0
    If (e & 1) <> 0
      acc = acc * base
    EndIf
    base = base * base
    e = e >> 1
  Wend
  If k < 0
    ProcedureReturn 1.0 / acc
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn acc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Pow(x, y) - x raised to the power y. There is no '^' operator in this
; language, which is what this function is for.
;
;     Pow(2, 10)    1024.0       Pow(0, 0)     1.0
;     Pow(-2, 3)    -8.0         Pow(0, -1)    +Infinity
;     Pow(-2, 0.5)  NaN          Pow(x, 0)     1.0, always
;     Pow(27, 0.3333333) about 3.0
;     Pow(-2, 100)  NaN          - NOT 1.27e30. See THE BOUND below.
;
; A NEGATIVE BASE NEEDS A WHOLE-NUMBER EXPONENT. Pow(-8, 1/3) is NaN
; here and NaN on the desktop: (-8)^(1/3) has no real value, and the
; cube root a reader is thinking of is a different function. This is a
; refusal, not a limitation.
;
; TWO PATHS, and which one you get changes the accuracy:
;
;   y is a whole number with |y| <= 64  ->  repeated squaring. Exact
;     where the answer is exact, 9 ULP worst case otherwise (MEASURED,
;     |y| <= 20 - see ACCURACY at the top), and this is where a negative
;     base is allowed. Pow(2,10) is exactly 1024.0.
;
;   otherwise  ->  Exp(y * Log(x)), which is the only general method
;     available and carries the error of the composition: about 7e-06
;     relative, roughly a hundred times the type's own resolution - that
;     is the 113 ULP in the ACCURACY table, read the other way round.
;     That is inherent - a 1-ULP error in Log(x) is multiplied by y
;     before Exp() ever sees it - and the whole-number path exists
;     precisely because it is the case where that price need not be paid.
;
; THE BOUND. For a POSITIVE base, |y| <= 64 is only a cost decision:
; x^64 already overflows binary32 for any base of 4 or more (4^64 is
; 2^128, which IS the overflow point), and below that the general path
; still answers, just less accurately.
;
; FOR A NEGATIVE BASE IT IS A CORRECTNESS BOUND, and that is the sharp
; edge in this function. Past |y| = 64 a negative base falls through to
; the general path, which refuses it - so Pow(-2, 100) is NaN even
; though the true answer, 1.2676506e30, is an ordinary representable
; binary32. MEASURED - on the RP2040 when this was written, and again
; here: rp2350LibSelfTest.pico2 asserts IsNaN(Pow(-2.0, 100.0)) = 1 on
; this target, so the sharp edge is confirmed on the Pico 2 build rather
; than inherited. If you need a negative base with a large whole
; exponent, call MathPowInt(x, k) directly: it has no bound and is what
; the short path uses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Pow(x.f, y.f)
  Protected k.l
  Protected kf.f
  Protected a.f
  Protected r.f
  If y = 0.0                        ; even Pow(NaN, 0) is 1.0 - the desktop agrees
    ProcedureReturn 1.0
  EndIf
  If x <> x
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If y <> y
    ProcedureReturn y
  EndIf
  If x = 1.0
    ProcedureReturn 1.0
  EndIf
  ; a whole-number exponent small enough to reach by squaring
  If y >= -64.0 And y <= 64.0
    k = Int(y)
    kf = k
    If kf = y
      If x = 0.0
        If k < 0
          ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2139095040)   ; +Infinity
        EndIf
        ProcedureReturn 0.0
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn MathPowInt(x, k)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If x = 0.0
    If y < 0.0
      ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2139095040)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  If x < 0.0
    ; a negative base with a fractional exponent has no real value
    ProcedureReturn FloatFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  a = Log(x)
  r = y * a
  ProcedureReturn Exp(r)
EndProcedure
